{
  "objective": "This study used the care cascade framework to compare health system performance for both conditions and assess associated socioeconomic disparities.",
  "methods": "This cross-sectional study analysed data from the Bangladesh Demographic and Health Survey 2022, including adults aged ≥18 years with biomarker measurements (n=14,296 for hypertension; n=13,980 for diabetes). Five-stage care cascades (prevalence, screening, diagnosis, treatment, control) were constructed for each condition. Survey-weighted descriptive and multivariable logistic regression were performed.",
  "results": "The weighted prevalence estimates were 22.1% for hypertension and 16.9% for diabetes. Both cascades revealed substantial attrition, but at different stages. For hypertension, screening was relatively high (78.3%), yet only 57.7% were diagnosed. Among diagnosed adults, 71.4% were receiving treatment, and among treated adults, 43.3% achieved blood pressure control. For diabetes, the main gap occurred at the initial stage of care: only 33.6% of adults had ever undergone blood glucose measurement, 33.5% of adults with diabetes had been diagnosed, 73.7% of diagnosed individuals received treatment, and 21.7% of treated individuals achieved glycaemic control. Socioeconomic disparities were evident, particularly for diabetes. Compared with the poorest, the richest had higher odds of diabetes screening (aOR 3.2, 95% CI: 2.7-3.9), diagnosis (aOR 3.0, 95% CI: 1.8–4.8), and treatment (aOR 5.0, 95% CI: 2.2–11.4), as well as hypertension screening (aOR 2.2, 95% CI: 1.7–3.0). Higher education was associated with a twofold increase in blood pressure control compared with no education, and improved screening and diagnosis for both conditions."
}
